I conjecture that open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object fails to distinguish two replies that its documented rule requires it to distinguish. The rule says: "Every top-level balanced object in the text is a candidate, not only the span from the first { to the last }: reasoning prose before or after the answer frequently contains stray braces, and an object nested inside another is not a candidate of its own." The two replies are: (1) '{"real":1} "{"fake":2}"' and (2) '{"real":2} "{"fake":2}"'. For reply (1), the rule requires the answer to be the object {'real': 1}, because the only top-level object is the first one; the second brace is inside a string and is not a top-level object. For reply (2), the rule requires the answer to be {'real': 2}. But the function returns {'fake': 2} for both, because its top-level scan treats the brace inside the string as a top-level object and, scanning in reverse, picks the later fake object. I would be wrong if the function actually returns {'real': 1} for reply (1) and {'real': 2} for reply (2), or if the rule is interpreted to count objects inside strings as top-level.